    "created_at": "2018-01-30T00:06:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-01-30T00:06:22Z",
    "body": "Just to be clear, Gecko implements the `<center>` behavior via a UA stylesheet and a `-moz-center` value for `text-align`.  So we don't have a problem with doing things like this via the UA sheet.\r\n\r\n`<div align>` is implemented as a presentation hint, not via the UA sheet, not least because it predates the ability to do case-insensitive attr value matching in Gecko's style system.  But even if we redid it on top of that, there would be performance to consider: `div[align=\"left\" i]` is a \"slow selector\" that you have to try to match for all div elements, getting attr values, etc.  Presentation hints are a lot easier to optimize to be fast...\r\n\r\nBut even ignoring all this, the reality of shipping a feature like this is that first it will get implemented, possibly behind a flag.  Then it will get shipped.  Then once it's clear that shipping is working and there is no web compat fallout that requires it to be turned off in a point release, critical features like parts of the UA sheet can get switched to using it.  So it should come as no surprise that initial implementation patches for something like this don't change the UA stylesheet.  If they did, that part would likely not pass code review.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
